{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789253667,"nonce":"ti0KqndbTaV/jFOjUPq6T7j/QSg=","title":"Python: import and a module story","message":"Nothing handles `import`. A program with any import fails, which rules\nout multi-file programs and every annotated file (`from typing import\nList` is the usual first line).\n\nNeeded:\n1. User modules: `import m`, `from m import f`, `import m as n`,\n   relative imports within a package. A module is a second Python file\n   lowered into the same TypedProgram with module-qualified names;\n   module globals already exist (ebf1e8d), so `m.x` is a qualified\n   global read.\n2. A stdlib map, decided per module: which of `math`, `random`, `time`,\n   `sys`, `os`, `collections`, `itertools`, `functools`, `typing`,\n   `string`, `re` become library functions in zyntax_builtins, which\n   go through a ZRTL plugin, and which are refused with a clear error.\n   `typing` should be accepted and mostly ignored: its names are\n   annotations the typer already understands.\n3. `__name__` and the `if __name__ == \"__main__\":` idiom.\n4. `sys.argv`, `sys.exit`, `sys.stdin`/`input()`, `math.*` first; they\n   are in nearly every script.","files":null}]}